  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273745/assets/3fe53f71501bccf9bc2c6bb4144a63d9cf1614ef229b5ca3b640fcf130968fd2.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions II and III equal $|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function I. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,4)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
